prompt:
You are a compliance referee. Explain this cross-department gap to a launch manager in 2-3 plain sentences and give 2-3 concrete fix options. Use ONLY these facts; do not invent any.
Seam type: data_residency_breach
Title: 2 supplier(s) process customer data outside UK/EEA, against the customer terms
Departments: Finance, Legal, Product
Facts: {"allowed_regions": ["UK", "EEA"], "breaches": ["V002 StreamLedger Inc: customer payment data processed in US-East (United States); C-2026-031 DPA signed=True", "V007 VerifyPoint Inc: customer identity data processed in US-East (United States); C-2026-045 DPA signed=True"], "promise": "Customer payment data is processed and stored only within the UK or EEA, including by sub-processors."}
Return JSON: {"explanation": str, "fix_options": [str]}

response:
```json
{
  "explanation": "Our customer terms promise that customer payment data is processed and stored only in the UK or EEA, including by sub-processors. However, two suppliers process customer data in US-East (United States): StreamLedger Inc (V002) handles customer payment data, and VerifyPoint Inc (V007) handles customer identity data. Both have signed DPAs (C-2026-031 and C-2026-045), but those agreements have not kept the processing within the allowed regions, so the gap sits across Finance, Legal and Product.",
  "fix_options": [
    "Move processing in-region: Product and Finance ask StreamLedger (V002) and VerifyPoint (V007) to switch customer data processing from US-East to a UK or EEA region, and confirm the change before launch.",
    "Replace the suppliers: Finance and Product move to alternative suppliers that process customer data only in the UK or EEA, starting with StreamLedger because the promise explicitly covers payment data.",
    "Align the terms with reality: Legal reviews the customer residency promise and decides whether it can be revised and communicated to customers. Until then, treat both suppliers' US-East processing as a breach that blocks launch."
  ]
}
```
